[Music]  [Applause]  dear jack on behalf of the admissions  committee is my pleasure to offer you  admission to the MIT class of 2010 this  was a letter received by a good friend  of mine who was at the time a high  school senior in Arizona it was an  incredible moment MIT was the school  that jacket dreamt of going to since  sophomore year he worked hard and had  paid off who remembers that feeling of  euphoria when you get something for  which you've long might be in your first  job or getting into university  but this notice was soon gone Jack's  parents returned home to find the letter  scrunched up and tossed onto the floor  in his place lay a second letter with  that same MIT branding set with a words  financial aid office written below dear  jack it began as before but over the  neatly typed writing below with a red  scroll you're not rich enough to go here  Jack had written this but he was right  what's worse is it happened again Jack  got into USC he literally had the letter  of acceptance in his hand but again they  left him with a $55,000 gap which he  couldn't cover intimidated by the  prospect of significant student debt he  eventually accepted a place a lower  ranked University where he qualified for  in-state fees now I chose this story  because it's a very standard one Jack  was from a middle-class family both his  parents had paying jobs and that's  exactly the point this isn't just a  problem that affects some families this  is a problem that affects the majority  indeed four out of ten students who get  accepted to their first choice  universities declined because they can't  afford to go there 4 out of 10 we all  know how expensive tuition is but for  context for Jack's parents whose two  kids want to go to private four-year  universities it would account for 40% of  their combined salaries every year post  PACs and it's only getting more  expensive over the last 20 years tuition  costs have increased a hundred percent  above inflation in that same time we  didn't found the income four percent  it's increased a hundred versus four so  is it any wonder that student debt is  now 1.3 trillion dollars more than  Russia's economy is it any wonder that  43 million Americans hold student loans  43 million and nearly 10 million of them  are in default and is it any wonder that  student loan payments have now surpassed  groceries as the biggest monthly  expenditure for many households  it's no wonder to me even though I come  from a comfortable family I'm part of  these statistics I took out the maximum  government loans or Amanda grad and I  now back in debt at Stanford is there  nothing we can do nothing we can do to  stop Jack scrunching up his admissions  letter  well actually 41 billion dollars is  donated every year by alumni to colleges  that's enough to cover the tuition of  4.5 million Jack's and these donations  are growing so where's the money going  it's not going to Jack stop going to  people that need it most it's going to  Stanford going to Harvard it's going to  Yale  all of which already have significant  endowment it just me result like giving  welfare support to billionaires and the  problem is these donations are having  less and less impact let me take an  example last year 92 University spent  one point seven billion dollars on  recreation park projects it's got so bad  the New York Times did an article solely  on aquatic features at universities a  couple of examples from that article one  Louisiana State University has just  finished building a lazy river that  spells out LSU the school director of  recreation yes they have a director of  recreation at a school when you ask the  public relations for approval sure they  said then yes but well thank y'all for  the but but only if it's in our  signature very task go font they also  spent three million dollars on a habitat  for their mascot Tiger Mike Clemson's  38-acre  lakefront property includes blobs yeah I  didn't know what blobs were either so I  asked they're like floating mattresses  so that and I quote the director  recreation so that students can jump  around like on American ninja now these  are just two examples from the article  but we really don't need to look far  from home the rumors are that Stanford  spends $40,000 on every palm tree so  next time you go down palm drive think  of every single palm tree as a student  that could not afford to go to this  institution  this is at best a real misuse of  donations and it worse  it's a recreation park arms race that's  driving up the cost of tuition now don't  get me wrong a beautiful setting can  create a wonderful learning environment  but it's no use of students can't afford  to go there I'm not saying don't donate  to colleges Stanford claims the Manistee  of all Mays and funded important  projects but I urge you to consider  donating equally to institutions and  individuals and you could do that by  creating scholarships they had the  highest marginal impact and it's the  most efficient way to support students  your money goes straight to their  tuition and that's why I spent the last  year building going Mary comm we make it  easy for any donor to create a  scholarship of any size and give that to  the perfect student who shares their  interests values or background anyone  can create a scholarship in less than  three minutes time you can change the  life of the student like Jack in less  time than it takes you to order an uber  President Obama once said that the u.s.  is not a country that guarantees equal  outcomes but we do strive to provide  everyone with an equal shot his  predecessors described this as at the  core of the American dream at the moment  frankly there isn't an equal shot but if  the donors in education stop funding  ninja blobs and start donating directly  to students then there can be and the  next time a student like Jack receives  an admission letter he can accept it  thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

